2.11

What's being used by the threat actor as a bootloader?

The attacker is using a legitimate program as a bootloader for persistence. Look at ProcessEvents for processes that start at boot time - the attacker hijacked a common Microsoft application to load their malware.

A legitimate Microsoft executable starts automatically at boot/logon and is abused to load malicious code.

previously we have this 

The idea is to see what the compromised username has on their ProcessEvents

start from the previous question with ""reg.exe save"" from cmd.exe /Q /c reg.exe save hklm\software C:\Temp\software.save 1> \\127.0.0.1\ADMIN$\__112335552221321_233321 2>&1
 and check whocht username was infected
After pivot that use the username and see what was other command used 
check all process without start with C:\

let A=
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "reg.exe save"
| distinct username;
ProcessEvents
| where username has_any (A)
| where process_commandline !contains @"C:\"
| distinct process_commandline

we know that a boot has a start command

